# Exercise 1 {.unnumbered}

Consider the following synthetic data:

```{r data_exercise_1}
n = 150
set.seed(43)
X1 <- runif(n,0,1)
set.seed(126)
Y <- (X1>0.8)*0.8*X1+(X1<0.8)*(X1>0.35)*(-0.5*X1-1)+(X1<0.35)*(X1-0.5)+rnorm(n,sd=0.2)
data_reg <- data.frame(X1=X1,Y=Y)
plot1<-ggplot(data_reg,aes(x=X1,y=Y))+geom_point(size=2, color="gray")+theme_classic()
plot1
summary(data_reg)
```

- **Question 1 [code + interpretation].** Have a look at the data and
  plot the data. *Guide: you can use the basic functions `head()`,
  `summary()` and `ggplot()`*. We want to explain the values taken by
  $Y$ by using the variable $X$. More precisely, we want to build a
  function $$\begin{array}{lccl}
  f : & [0;1] & \longrightarrow & \mathbb R \\
    & x & \longmapsto & f(x)=\hat{y} \end{array}$$

  that for any value $x$ for $X$ provides a prediction $f(x)=\hat{y}$
  for $Y$. What kind of problem is it?

  *Regression problem*

- **Question 2 [interpretation].** To find the function $f$, we will
  apply CART on the data. In your opinion, how many splits could the
  CART tree have ? *3*

- **Question 3 [code].** Fit a CART tree to explain $Y$ based on $X$ by
