- **Question 6 [interpretation + code].** Moreover, we know that
  $$ y_{151} = 0.4 \quad y_{152} = 0.57 \quad y_{153} = 0.8$$
  - Give the formulas for the quadratic, absolute, and HUBER loss
    functions. What other loss functions can be used in regression
    analysis?
  - For each of the three previous loss fonction (quadratic, absolute
    and HUBER (with delta= 1)). Use `R` to compute the value of the
    associate empirical risk evaluate on the the three new observations.
    Use `R` to confirm your previous results..

$$\mathcal{L}^2=|y_i - \hat{y_i}|^2 \quad 
\mathcal{L}=|y_i - \hat{y_i}| \quad 
\mathcal{L}_{HUBER} =
\left \{
\begin{array}{rcl}
\frac{1}{2}(y-\hat{y})^2 \text{for} |y-\hat{y}|\leq\delta\\
\delta\times(|y-\hat{y}|-\frac{1}{2}\delta) \text{otherwise}
\end{array}
\right.$$

```{r}
new_obs <- c(0.4,0.57,0.8)
MSE <- mean(cbind(prediction, new_obs),1,function(x) ((x[1]-x[2])^2)); MSE
```

# Exercise 2 {.unnumbered}

Consider the following synthetic data with two inputs continuous
variables $X_1$ and $X_2$ and the output variable $Y$ which takes value
in ${0,1}$. We want to explain the values taken by $Y$ by using the
variable $\bX = (X_1,X_2)$. More precisely, we want to build a function
$$\begin{array}{lccl}
f : & [-1;1]^2 & \longrightarrow & \lbrace 0,1 \rbrace \\
    & (x_1,x_2) & \longmapsto & f(x_1,x_2)=\hat y \end{array}$$

that for any value $\bx = (x_1,x_2)$ for $\bX$ provides a prediction
$f(\bx)=\hat y$ for $Y$.

```{r data_exercise_2}
n = 150 # Number of observations
set.seed(43) # random number generator, useful for creating simulations or 
#random objects that can be reproduced.

X1 <- runif(n,-1,1) # generates n uniform random numbers between 0 and 1
set.seed(126)
X2 <- runif(n,-1,1)
Y <- rep(0,n)
ind<-which((X1<=-0.15)|(X1>-0.15 & X2<=0.25))
set.seed(54)
Y[ind] <- rbinom(n=length(ind),size=1,p=0.9) # generates n bernouilli 
# distributed random variables  with p=0.9

set.seed(52432)
Y[setdiff(1:n,ind)]<- rbinom(n-length(ind),1,0.1)
data_class1 <- data.frame(X1=X1,X2=X2,Y=Y)
y <- as.factor(Y)
```

- **Question 1 [code + interpretation].** Have a look at the data and
  plot the data.

```{r}
plot1<-ggplot(data_class1,aes(x=X1,y=X2))+geom_point(aes(shape=y, color=y), size=3)+theme_classic()
plot1
```

- What kind of problem is it?
  - Classification
- How many splits would you like to use to partition the data space ?

```{r}
tree <- rpart(Y ~ . , data = data_class1, method ="class")
plotcp(tree)
printcp(tree)
```

According to the 1-SE rule, we will choose a tree with 3 splits

```{r}
T1 <- prune(tree, cp = 0.024351)
rpart.plot(T1)
```

